<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Emirate of Afghanistan</h1><p class="st-meta">1823 CE – 1926 CE · capital Kabul</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Barakzai state that reunited Afghanistan, fought two wars with Britain and had its borders fixed by the Great Game.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the collapse of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadozai">Sadozai</a> power, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dost-mohammad-khan">Dost Mohammad Khan</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barakzai-dynasty">Barakzai</a> clan took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> in 1826 and assumed the title emir in the mid-1830s, building on Barakzai control from 1823. He was deposed by a British invasion in 1839 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-afghan-war">First Anglo-Afghan War</a> but returned in 1842 and later reunited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> ruled a fragmented tribal society, with power resting on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashtuns">Pashtun</a> tribal chiefs. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-afghan-war">Second Anglo-Afghan War</a> of 1878 to 1880, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdur-rahman-khan">Abdur Rahman Khan</a>, the 'Iron Emir', centralised power, crushed revolts and annexed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hazarajat">Hazarajat</a>, while <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> controlled foreign policy. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durand-line">Durand Line</a> of 1893 and Anglo-Russian agreements set the borders.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1919 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amanullah-khan">Amanullah Khan</a> fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-anglo-afghan-war">Third Anglo-Afghan War</a> and won full independence. In 1926 he took the title king, turning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-afghanistan">Kingdom of Afghanistan</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/emirate-of-afghanistan.jpg" alt="Emirate of Afghanistan" width="320" height="196"><figcaption>Image: QWerk. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Afghanistan_(1901%E2%80%931919).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1823 CE – 1926 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kabul</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Dost Mohammad Khan, Abdur Rahman Khan, Habibullah Khan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Pashto, Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Emirate of Kabul, Barakzai Emirate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1874&amp;focus=emirate-of-afghanistan" aria-label="Emirate of Afghanistan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6437 927 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Emirate of Afghanistan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6757 1183L6757 1183M6757 1183L6582 1190M6757 1183L6695 1282M6757 1183L4997 651M6757 1183L6757 1183M6757 1183L6729 1110"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6757 1183h0M6582 1190h0M6695 1282h0M4997 651h0M6757 1183h0M6729 1110h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6757 1183h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6757 1183h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kabul. <a href="/atlas?year=1874&amp;focus=emirate-of-afghanistan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
